            size_t pos = (start + i) % table->capacity.load(memory_order_relaxed);
            Entry& entry = table->table.load(memory_order_relaxed)[pos];

            uint32_t expected = EMPTY;
            if (entry.state.compare_exchange_weak(expected, OCCUPIED, 
                                                memory_order_acq_rel)) {
                entry.key.store(k, memory_order_release);
                entry.value.store(v, memory_order_release);
                return true;
            }

            if (expected == OCCUPIED && 
                entry.key.load(memory_order_acquire) == k) {
                return false; // duplicate key
            }
        }

        // Trigger resize if needed
        if (should_resize(table)) {
            help_resize(table);
        }
    }
}
```

## Resize Without Blocking Readers

Use a **helping mechanism**:
1. When load factor exceeds 75%, allocating thread creates new table
2. Set `next` pointer to new table with `memory_order_release`  
3. All threads help migrate entries from old to new table
4. Readers can continue using old table until migration completes
5. Once migration done, atomically switch table pointer

**Migration ensures:**
- Each entry migrated exactly once using CAS on migration state
- Readers see consistent view (either old complete table or new complete table)
- No blocking - readers always make progress

